  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nTONIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT:\n\nThe cold front will progress farther southeast across the area \ntonight. Winds will remain gusty into early evening and may be \ngusty all night as cold air advection develops. The wind will \nkeep temperatures from falling too far. Lows tonight will be in \nthe low to upper 40s.\n\nA stronger surge of cold air advection arrives Wednesday along \nwith a northwesterly low-level jet. Diurnal heating will \nsupport wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph. Scattered to isolated rain \nshowers may develop during the afternoon in response to the \nheating beneath colder air aloft. The best chance of a few \nsprinkles, at least, is found over the Arrowhead and portions of\nnorthwest Wisconsin. Temperatures will be a little cooler than \ntoday, though still a few degrees above normal, with highs in \nthe upper 50s to upper 60s. Winds will diminish slightly \nWednesday night and may remain gusty as cold air advection \npersists.\n\nThursday will see temps another few degrees cooler than \nWednesday with highs in the middle 50s to middle 60s. Northwest \nwinds will not be as strong. Friday temperatures trend a few \ndegrees warmer.\n\nSATURDAY INTO NEXT WEEK:\n\nHeading into the weekend the upper-level pattern is expected to\ntransition from split flow with a quasi-zonal wind field over \nthe northern Plains and Canadian Prairies to a southwesterly \npattern with a ridge building over the eastern Great Lakes. \nSurface low pressure is expected to develop in the lee of the \nCanadian Rockies Friday as a longwave trough digs into the \nnorthern US Rockies.\n\nWarm and moist air advection will develop over the Upper \nMidwest Saturday morning which will result in well above normal \ntemperatures. Highs in the middle to upper 60s are expected over\nthe eastern Arrowhead with low to middle 70s over north-central\nand portions of central Minnesota. The warmest temperatures \nwill be in the I-35 corridor east into northwest Wisconsin where\nupper 70s and low 80s are forecast as of this afternoon. The \nstrongest isentropic ascent will be north of the area over \nnorthwest Ontario and precipitation chances are low.\n\nA cold front will move into the region Sunday. Ahead of the \nfront PWATs will increase to 0.75-1.3 inches due to the \npersistent theta-e advection in the southwest flow aloft. Rain \nchances will increase for the second half of the weekend in \nresponse to the ascent provided by the cold front. The front is \nforecast to stall over the Upper Midwest keeping isentropic lift\nand rain chances in the picture Monday and Tuesday.",
